Since you're on .NET 10, you should use the **built-in `TimeProvider`** — no custom wrapper needed.

## Adopt `TimeProvider`

**1. Inject `TimeProvider` into `ReportGenerator`:**

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string GenerateReport(string reportName)
    {
        var timestamp = timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("yyyyMMdd_HHmmss");
        // ...
        var content = $"Report: {reportName}\nGenerated: {timeProvider.GetUtcNow():O}\n...";
        // ...
    }
}
```

**2. Register in DI (Program.cs):**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

**3. Mock in tests** — install `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`:

```
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

```csharp
var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(
    new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 25, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var path = generator.GenerateReport("Sales");

Assert.Contains("20260625_120000", path); // deterministic!
```

---

`TimeProvider.GetUtcNow()` returns `DateTimeOffset`. If you need a `DateTime`, use `.UtcDateTime`. The `FakeTimeProvider` also supports `Advance(TimeSpan)` to simulate time passing.

> **Note:** Your class also uses `File.*`, `Directory.*`, `Environment.*`, and `Console.*` — those are additional static dependencies that would need separate wrappers (e.g., `System.IO.Abstractions` for file I/O) to make the class fully testable. Want me to help with those too?
